 1 big thing: Beef&#x27;s growing beef 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Shoshana Gordon/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump ramped up his effort to lower the price of beef today, saying he would temporarily increase imports free of tariffs — and that he had secured a commitment to have the meat sold at 25% below current market prices.

 "As we work to rebuild this herd and help our ranchers, for the next 90 days, the United States will allow up to 300,000 metric tons of product for ground beef to be imported with no out of quota tariff," he said on Truth Social . Zoom in: A White House official said the commitment came from foreign beef exporters and that the 25% discount would be passed along to U.S. consumers.

 🏷️ Yes, but: Exporters typically do not control retail prices — retailers do — so it was not immediately clear how the president can guarantee the discounts will flow through to consumers.

 "This short-term action allows foreign imports to fill a gap to meet domestic ground beef demand at affordable prices," the White House official said. 🐂 Friction point: A U.S. cattle industry trade group said Friday it was "disappointed" by Trump's announcement.

 "While America's cattle producers share the goal of keeping groceries affordable for consumers, flooding the market with government-subsidized, below-market beef is not the way to rebuild the American cattle herd," Colin Woodall, CEO of the National Cattlemen's Beef Association, said in a statement. What's next: Trump will sign an executive order authorizing the tariff-free imports within the next two weeks, the White House official said.

